course: chinese-street
lesson: 19
title: Animals and "what animal are you?"
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Lín asks "nǐ shǔ shénme?" — "nee SHOO SHUN-muh", literally "what do you belong to?" — and he means your zodiac animal, the animal of the Chinese year you were born in.
explain: The twelve animals go in order: Rat, Ox, Tiger, Rabbit, Dragon, Snake, Horse, Goat, Monkey, Rooster, Dog, Pig; the year turns at Chinese New Year, not on 1 January, so a January birthday may belong to the year before.
explain: Animal names are full of surprises: xióngmāo, panda, is "bear-cat"; lǎohǔ, tiger, and lǎoshǔ, mouse, both start with lǎo, "old", but nothing about them is old.
explain: Tones turn animals into other things: māo (cat) is high and level, but máo rising is fur; niú (cow) is also slang for "awesome!"; yú (fish) sounds like the word for "plenty", so a whole fish is eaten at New Year.
explain: Chinese dragons, lóng, are lucky and wise and bring rain — not the fire-breathing baddies of English stories.
fact: "gǒu" (dipping) means dog; xiǎo gǒu (puppy) is sweet, but calling a person a dog is a real insult.
fact: "māo" (high and level) means cat; máo (rising) means fur, and also the "10p" coin of Chinese money.
fact: "xióngmāo" means panda, literally "bear-cat"; in Taiwan some people say māoxióng.
fact: "lǎohǔ" means tiger and "lǎoshǔ" means mouse or rat; the lǎo (old) in them does not mean old.
fact: "hóuzi" means monkey; the Monkey King of the stories is Sūn Wùkōng, and "nǐ zhēn hóu!" teases a cheeky, fidgety kid.
fact: "tùzi" (falling) means rabbit, and a Moon Rabbit appears in the Mid-Autumn story; tǔ (dipping) means soil.
fact: "zhū" (high) means pig; Peppa Pig is Xiǎo Zhū Pèiqí, and "bèn zhū" (stupid pig) is light between friends but an insult from a stranger.
fact: "niú" (rising) means cow or ox, and in slang "tài niú le!" means "awesome!".
fact: "yú" (rising) means fish; it sounds like the word for "surplus", so a whole fish is eaten at New Year for "plenty every year", and some families never flip the fish over, like a boat capsizing.
fact: "lóng" (rising) means dragon; Chinese dragons are lucky, wise and bring rain.
fact: "nǐ shǔ shénme?" asks your zodiac animal; you answer like "wǒ shǔ lóng", I'm a Dragon.
fact: The twelve zodiac animals in order are Rat, Ox, Tiger, Rabbit, Dragon, Snake, Horse, Goat, Monkey, Rooster, Dog and Pig; the Rat comes first and is thought clever.
fact: The zodiac year begins at Chinese New Year (late January or February), so someone born in early January belongs to the year before.
exercise: Lín's dog barks at the gate. What is a dog in Mandarin? | gǒu | māo | zhū | niú
exercise: You hear "maow", high and level. What animal is it? | Cat | Fur (máo) | Panda | Mouse
exercise: What is xióngmāo word for word? | Bear-cat | Cat-bear | Big cat | Black-white cat
exercise: At the zoo Lín points to a big striped cat: "lǎohǔ!". What is it? | A tiger | An old cat | A big mouse | A young lion
exercise: Lín screams "lǎoshǔ!" in the kitchen. What did he see? | A mouse | A big tiger | An old man | A rabbit
exercise: Who is Sūn Wùkōng? | The Monkey King of the stories | A famous panda at the zoo | The first animal of the zodiac | A dragon that brings rain
exercise: Lín's granny calls him "nǐ zhēn hóu!" when he can't sit still. What is she saying? | You're such a cheeky monkey | You're such a lazy pig | You're such a scaredy-cat | You're such a clever dragon
exercise: What animal is tùzi? | Rabbit | Little monkey | Garden soil | Baby pig
exercise: Peppa Pig on Chinese TV is "Xiǎo Zhū Pèiqí". What does zhū mean? | Pig | Cow | Dog | Pink
exercise: Lín's mate calls him "bèn zhū" with a grin after he misses a goal. How serious is it? | Light teasing between friends | A terrible insult every time | A compliment for good play | A word for a goalkeeper
exercise: What farm animal is niú? | Cow or ox | Pig | Goat | Horse
exercise: Lín's mum serves a whole fish at New Year. Why a whole one? | yú sounds like "plenty", for plenty every year | Fish is the cheapest meat | Granny can't use a knife | A whole fish is easier to cook
exercise: Lín's granny tells a story about a lóng. What's it like? | Lucky and wise, it brings rain | Fire-breathing and evil | A small grey lizard | A sea monster that eats boats
exercise: Lín asks "nǐ shǔ shénme?". What does he want to know? | Your zodiac animal | Your favourite animal | Your pet's name | What you're scared of
exercise: You were born in a Dragon year. What do you answer? | wǒ shǔ lóng | wǒ shì lǎohǔ | wǒ xǐhuan lóng | wǒ jiào lóng
exercise: Which animal comes first in the Chinese zodiac? | The Rat | The Dragon | The Ox (niú) | The Tiger
exercise: When does the zodiac year begin? | At Chinese New Year, late Jan or Feb | On 1 January every year, like ours | On your own birthday each year | At the Mid-Autumn Festival
test: lol Lín's a Rat in the zodiac, that's an insult innit? | No, the Rat is clever and comes first | Yes, rats are always an insult, everyone knows | Yes, it means he's dirty | No, Rat means "boss"
test: Your friend was born on 10 January. Lín says his animal is from the year before. Why? | The zodiac year starts at Chinese New Year, not 1 January | Lín just got it wrong, the animal changes on 1 January | January babies have no animal | Animals change on 1 January
test: You meant "cat" but your voice rose: "máo". What might Lín hear? | Fur, or the 10p coin | A cat, nothing changes | A tiger | A panda
test: Lín says "tài niú le!" when you do a kickflip. Is he calling you a cow? | No, it's slang for "awesome!" | Yes, it means "you're a cow" | Yes, it means "you're fat" | No, it means "too slow"
test: In a British cartoon a dragon burns a village. Lín's granny says Chinese dragons aren't like that. What's she right about? | Chinese dragons are lucky, wise rain-bringers | Chinese dragons are even scarier and burn cities | Chinese dragons live only in deep caves | Chinese dragons are always small
test: At the New Year dinner you start to flip the fish to get the meat underneath. What might Lín's granny say? | Some families don't flip it, like a boat capsizing | Flip it twice for double luck | Eat the head first | Throw the bones on the floor
test: A stranger calls Lín a dog in an angry voice. How serious is that? | It's a real insult | It's a friendly joke | It's a compliment | It's a zodiac question
test: Lín mixes up tùzi (falling) and tǔ (dipping). What did he nearly say? | Soil instead of rabbit | Pig instead of rabbit | Monkey instead of rabbit | Moon instead of rabbit
test: Lín is a Tiger. In the zodiac order, which animal's year comes right after the Tiger? | Rabbit | Dragon | Rat | Snake
test: Lín swears lǎohǔ means "old tiger" because lǎo means old. Is that right? | No, lǎohǔ is just "tiger" | Yes, it's an old tiger only | Yes, young tigers are xiǎohǔ only | No, it means "old mouse"
test: Which pair both start with lǎo but have nothing to do with being old? | lǎohǔ and lǎoshǔ | lóng and niú | xióngmāo and hóuzi | tùzi and zhū
exam: Identify the literal sense of the Mandarin term "xióngmāo", denoting the giant panda. | Bear-cat | Cat-bear | Great cat | Spotted bear
exam: It is given that an individual was born on the fifth day of January. Select the statement consistent with the established conventions of the Chinese zodiac. | He may belong to the preceding year's animal, the year turning at Chinese New Year | He invariably belongs to the new year's animal, the year turning on the first of January | The individual is assigned no animal | The animal is determined by the month of birth alone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dragon (lóng) in Chinese tradition? | It is auspicious and wise, associated with bringing rain | It is a malevolent fire-breathing beast that destroys villages | It is a creature of ill omen | It is a minor domestic animal
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a whole fish is customarily served at the New Year meal. | The word yú is homophonous with "surplus", betokening plenty each year | Fish is prescribed by law at New Year | Fish is the sole meat available in winter | The fish represents the Rat of the zodiac
